      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
During the blower door test, we identified that your house has some air leakage associated with the following locations:

** The current 7" and 8" chimney for the furnace and wood stove.
** Fair bit of air leakage is coming through the frames of the old windows - some of the slider windows are not sealing properly. I also suspect there is pink fibreglass insulation behind the frames of the old windows, pink fibreglass insulation is a good insulator but a poor air sealer. I would recommend adding spray foam insulation whenever you are replacing the older windows.
** The outside doors - these doors are not sealing properly (can seal with weatherstripping).
** Some of the electrical outlets in exterior walls of the house - e.g. the one near the entrance door. Foam wall plate gaskets can be installed behind the plates.

Savings: I modelled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 220 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 7 GJ, and qualify for $ 500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). Achieving a minimum 20% reduction qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.

Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>
The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation and garage wall appears to be 2x4 with R12 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R15 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding.

Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).

Savings:
By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 610 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 18.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose to add a minimum R10, your rebate &amp; energy savings will decrease or if you choose to add a minimum R20, your rebate &amp; energy savings will increase.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $4,940 is available for adding R-15 insulation to the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.</mainWalls>
                <foundation>
The basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with inconsistent insulation. I have modeled adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation, as well as 2x4 framing with 3.5 inches (R14) of batt insulation. I have also modelled adding 4 inches (R20) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation to the basement header area.

Savings: By adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation and 2x4 framing with 3.5 inches (R14) of fiberglass batt insulation to the basement walls, and adding 4 inches (R20) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation to the basement header area, you could potentially save $ 1,400 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 44 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 2,500.00 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade. By sealing and insulating your entire basement header area by a minimum of RSI 3.52 (R-20), you could qualify for up to $ 500 rebate from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP)

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to a $ 1,250.00 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program and $250 for the basement header area. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

The Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative offers grants of up to $1,950 for adding R24 insulation to basement walls. Insulating basement walls can reduce energy costs and improve comfort. Grants are based on the RSI/R-value of the added insulation and the percentage of wall area insulated. The current amount of insulation does not affect the grant amounts. A minimum of 20% of the exterior foundation wall area must be insulated.

Basement Insulation - Header: Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $240 is available for sealing and adding insulation to basement headers. In an unfinished basement, sealing and insulating headers can be a relatively simple task. The grant of $240 is available when RSI 3.52 (R-20) or more is added to a minimum of 80% of your entire basement header area, along with air sealing.</foundation>
                <windows>
Most of the windows in this house (13 total between the basement and main floor) are older double pane windows, that you may choose to upgrade. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STARÂ® certified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.

Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 150 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $3,000 maximum in rebates (12 existing windows @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.

Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for window upgrades.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the Searchable product list of eligible windows for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.

ENERGY STAR labels must be affixed to windows at the time of installation and remain in place until the time of your post-retrofit evaluation. If ENERGY STAR labels are removed prior to the post-retrofit evaluation, the following photos are your responsibility to acquire, retain and provide to the energy advisor at the time of the post-retrofit evaluation:
A photo of each newly-installed window showing a portion of the surrounding wall assembly from the inside, outside or both, AND

A photo of each ENERGY STAR label affixed to the window that clearly shows the ENERGY STAR certification mark, Manufacturer model code/number, U-factor/Energy Rating, NRCan reference number or a Certified Products Directory (CPD) number, and a Certified mark from one of the following:
? Canadian Standards Association (CSA)
? Intertek Canada
? Keystone Certifications
? Labtest Certification (LC)
? QAI Laboratories
? National Fenestration Council (NFRC)

Removed labels must be submitted and provided to the energy advisor.

Request your supplier/installer to include the following information on the invoice:
? Name of the homeowner and address of the house;
? Date of installation;
? Window make and model numbers;
? NRCan Reference Number;
? Indication of whether windows are ENERGY STAR or ENERGY STAR Most Efficient certified;
? Number of windows and location of each installation;
? U-factor/Energy Rating (ER);
? Itemized cost of each window and total cost of installation; and
? Itemization of any other work done as part of the installation, such as air/gap sealing and associated costs.

Please note that a window schedule (quote) cannot be used as a substitute for the aforementioned acceptable window validations and cannot be submitted to the portal as a standalone document to validate window installation and eligibility. However, the window schedule may be attached to the invoice provided there is a reference on the invoice to a schedule reference number to link the two documents.</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>
You currently have a 121 litre oil-fired hot water tank. You may want to consider replacing your oil-fired hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.

Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, could potentially save $ 280 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your main floor of the house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient oil furnace, propane fireplace and a Blaze King wood stove indirectly through the stairs. Your basement suite is heated with a 77% efficient hot water tank through a fan coil unit. 

In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (adding more insulation and reducing the air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade for the main floor of the house to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat and installing a dedicated combustion air inlet for the wood stove. I have also modelled an upgrade for the basement to an ENERGY STAR qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE.

Savings: By replacing your current heating system with the ones mentioned above, you could potentially save $ 1,720 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 46 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 4,500 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). 

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 2,250 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler. To determine the eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that the unit is included on the Searchable product list of eligible furnaces or boilers for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Wood Stove:

I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of propane saved from a new high efficiency furnace and the cost of wood in Fort Simpson at the $100/cord "self-harvested" rate) if you use your current wood stove, used for about 50% of the heating requirement for the main floor of your house. Getting that wood up to the main floor is something your heating contractor can work on by using the new furnace fan to circulate the wood heat throughout the house. I modeled an upgrade that has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe.

Savings: By using your current 81% efficient wood stove for 50% of the heating requirements of your house, you could potentially save $ 2,700 per year in energy costs, in addition to the energy savings previously outlined for installing the 97% AFUE propane furnace with the ECM blower motor and 95% AFUE propane boiler. 

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>
Your house has an older 13 liter toilet that should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush!

Savings: By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>
Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.

DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:
      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or
      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available

For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $19,000 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:

     - $5,000 for the wall insulation
     - $3,000 for the windows
     - $3,000 for the foundation insulation
     - $4,500 for the heating system
     - $500 for the air leakage reduction
     - $3,000 for the bonus rebate

NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than you are not eligible for the DHERP - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the foundation insulation and the heating system, however these would only be 50% of what is listed above.

In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:
     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $250
     - Programmable Thermostat - 50% up to $150
     - LED Lighting - see section further down

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:
- In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
- If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
- Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
- All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
- It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
--- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
--- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
--- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
- All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.
Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights Â Non-hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Fort Simpson, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Fail

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood, bathroom fans and clothes dryers), indicated a pressure drop of 7.4 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace, domestic hot water tanks and wood stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting. 

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house: 
        - a range hood fan
        - a bathroom fan
        - two clothes dryer

You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs Â Fort Simpson

The following Dec. 1, 2022 values, excluding GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.00 min, $0.31 per kWh up to subsidy limit, after subsidy $0.69 per kWh
Oil - $1.23 per liter 
Propane - $0.79 per liter delivered
Wood - $400 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $6.57 per bag, $328 per tonne bulk delivered

** Oil &amp; Biomass fuels in Fort Simpson tend to experience cyclical fuel price volatility. The Arctic Energy Alliance therefore uses 5-year rolling average for these values to better help you evaluate longer term heating appliance purchase/upgrade decisions.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

